.TH DARPA-SPILL-PUBLISH 1 "October 2026" "darpa_spillserver 1.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
darpa-spill-publish \- dump, shift, upload and report spill data, from cron
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B darpa-spill-publish
.RI [ COMMON ]
.B hourly
.RB [ \-\-hour
.IR TIME ]
.PP
.B darpa-spill-publish
.RI [ COMMON ]
.B daily
.RB [ \-\-date
.IR YYYY\-MM\-DD ]
.PP
.B darpa-spill-publish
.RI [ COMMON ]
.B backfill
.BI \-\-start " TIME"
.BI \-\-end " TIME"
.RB [ \-\-hourly | \-\-no\-hourly ]
.RB [ \-\-daily | \-\-no\-daily ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-upload ]
.PP
.B darpa-spill-publish
.RI [ COMMON ]
.B token
.RB [ \-\-month
.IR YYYY\-MM " |"
.BR \-\-next ]
.PP
.B darpa-spill-publish
.RI [ COMMON ]
.BR upload " | " creds " | " status " | " check
.PP
.B darpa-spill-publish
.BR \-V " | " \-h
.PP
.I COMMON
is
.RB [ \-c
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-set
.IR SECTION.KEY = VALUE " ...]"
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-v | \-q ],
accepted before or after the subcommand.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B darpa-spill-publish
turns the archive behind a running
.BR darpa-spill-server (1)
into published files on the Near Detector gateway.  cron runs it; each run
carries its files through four stages, each feeding the next:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dump  \->  shift  \->  upload  \->  status
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B dump
.BR darpa-spill-dump (1)
.B \-\-split hour
or
.B \-\-split day
against the server, once per configured stream (by default
.B numi
=
.BR "\-\-signal $74 \-\-signal $A9" ,
.B all
= every event,
.B booster
=
.BR "\-\-type BNB \-\-type BNB_TCLK" ),
writing CSV and JSON from one fetch into the
.B raw
tree.  Empty pieces are kept, so a gap in the beam is visible as an empty
file rather than a missing one.
.TP
.B shift
.B darpa-spill-shift apply
(see
.BR darpa-spill-shift (1))
writes a blurred copy of every raw file into the
.B shifted
tree, using the
.B dts1
token of the month the
.I data
belongs to, not the month the job runs in.  An event that appears in an
hourly and a daily file is then shifted identically in both, and the two
copies cannot be averaged against each other.  A missing token is created
(never overwritten) and an alert asks for it to be backed up.
.TP
.B upload
.B fdp\-put \-\-skip\-existing
copies raw files to
.B fdp.raw_dest
and shifted files to
.B fdp.shifted_dest
on the Fermi Data Platform, keeping the local directory layout.  It runs
inside
.BR fdp.env_script ,
grouped by destination directory, at most 200 files per call.
.TP
.B status
writes
.I index.html
and
.I status.json
into
.BR paths.status_dir ,
which
.BR darpa-spill-server (1)
serves at
.I /status/
when its
.B server.status_dir
points there.
.SS Layout
Hour and day boundaries, labels and directory dates are in
.B timezone
(America/Chicago).  For a piece starting on local date YYYY-MM-DD:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{data_root}/raw/{mon}/{year}\-{mon}\-{day}/{prefix}\-{stream}\-{label}.{ext}
{data_root}/shifted/{mon}/{year}\-{mon}\-{day}/{prefix}\-{stream}\-{label}.shifted.{ext}
.fi
.RE
.PP
where
.I {mon}
is
.BR jan " ... " dec
(independent of the locale), the hourly label is the local hour and its UTC
offset
.RB ( 2026\-10\-22T13\-0500 )
and the daily label the local date
.RB ( 2026\-10\-22 ).
For example
.IR raw/oct/2026\-oct\-22/nd\-numi\-2026\-10\-22T13\-0500.csv .
On daylight-saving changeover days the daily file covers 23 or 25 hours, and
the repeated autumn hour is two files told apart by their offsets
.RB ( T01\-0500 " and " T01\-0600 ).
Labels and paths are computed by the same code
.BR darpa-spill-dump (1)
uses, so the two cannot disagree.
.PP
The remote directory is the destination root plus the same
.I {mon}/{year}\-{mon}\-{day}
path, e.g.
.IR /amsc/novaend/raw/oct/2026\-oct\-22/ " and"
.IR /amsc/public/novaend/shifted/oct/2026\-oct\-22/ .
.SS State
Every file's progress (dumped, rows, bytes, shifted, raw uploaded, shifted
uploaded, last error) is a row in the SQLite database
.IR {state_dir}/publish.db .
Any stage can fail and be finished by a later run.  A raw file that is
recorded, or that is found complete on disk, is never dumped again; files are
never replaced, here or on FDP, where dCache forbids overwriting.
.SS Ingest gate
Before a piece
.RI [ start ", " end )
is dumped, every enabled source in
.I /api/sources
must have been read up to
.IR end :
either its newest stored event
.RB ( watermark_nova )
or the start of its last complete poll
.RB ( polled_through_nova ,
less
.B ingest_check.settle
seconds) has reached it.  The second lets a quiet beam (shutdown,
maintenance, a silent TDU) through: its newest event stops, its polls go on,
and the empty files are written.  A source that is enabled but not running,
or has not finished a poll since the server started, holds the gate.
The tool polls every
.B ingest_check.poll
seconds for up to
.B ingest_check.wait
seconds; pieces still not covered are left for the next run, an
.B ingest not ready
alert is sent and the exit status is 75.  Pieces that are covered are still
processed.  A piece that leaves the catch-up window
.RB ( hourly.catchup_hours ,
.BR daily.catchup_days )
without being completed is mailed once, with the
.B backfill
command that redoes it.
.SS Alerts
Problems are mailed with
.B alerts.mail_command
.RB ( mailx )
as
.BI "\(dq[darpa-spill-publish] " subject \(dq
to
.BR alerts.email .
A condition is mailed at once, then again after
.B alerts.min_interval
seconds, twice that, four times, ... up to
.BR alerts.max_interval ;
the back-off resets when the condition clears (ingest ready, an upload or
dump succeeds, a valid ticket, a token refresh).  An authentication failure
of
.B fdp\-put
is not mailed while a Kerberos or token alert is already out.  When the
state database cannot be written (a full disk) the mail still goes,
rate-limited by a stamp file in the temporary directory.  A failing mailer is
logged and never ends the run.
.PP
Alerts go out from any host the tool runs on.  Trying it off the gateway
with the example configuration, add
.BR "\-\-set alerts.enabled=false" .
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
Every subcommand runs under the lock
.IR {state_dir}/ COMMAND .lock;
if the same subcommand is already running it exits 75 at once, and mails
when the holder has run longer than
.B alerts.stale_job_hours
(a hung job; not for
.BR backfill ).
.BR hourly ,
.B daily
and
.B backfill
also share
.I {state_dir}/dump.lock
around their dump and shift stage, waited for up to 30 minutes, so two of
them never write the same piece at once; a run that cannot get it exits 75
(result
.BR busy ).
Every
subcommand re-renders the status page at the end and records a row in the
.B runs
table.
.TP
.B hourly
Dump, shift and upload the preceding local hour, and any hour of the last
.B hourly.catchup_hours
(24) that is not complete (dumped and shifted for every stream and format).
Missing hours that abut are dumped with one
.B darpa-spill-dump
call per stream.
.TP
.BI "hourly \-\-hour " TIME
Process the hour beginning at
.I TIME
as though run in the hour after it, catch-up included.
.I TIME
is a label
.RB ( 2026\-11\-01T01\-0600 ),
a bare local hour
.RB ( 2026\-10\-22T13 ),
or any time expression
.BR darpa-spill-query (1)
accepts; a naive time is local.  Use the label to choose one of the two
repeated autumn hours.
.TP
.B daily
Dump, shift and upload the preceding local day, and any of the
.B daily.catchup_days
(3) days before it that is not complete.
.TP
.BI "daily \-\-date " YYYY\-MM\-DD
Process that local day (and the catch-up days before it) instead.
.TP
.B backfill
Process every whole hour and every whole day in
.BI \-\-start " TIME"
to
.BI \-\-end " TIME"
(half-open; a date-only end includes that day; the end is clamped to now).
Partial hours or days at either end are dropped, since they would produce a
short file under a full-period name.  One
.B darpa-spill-dump
call per stream per kind covers each contiguous run of missing pieces.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-hourly ", " \-\-no\-hourly
Hourly files (default: yes).
.TP
.BR \-\-daily ", " \-\-no\-daily
Daily files (default: yes).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-upload
Dump and shift only; a later
.B upload
sends the files.
.RE
.TP
.B upload
Upload every raw and shifted file not yet recorded as uploaded.  Holds
.IR {state_dir}/upload.lock ;
the upload stage of
.BR hourly ", " daily " and " backfill
waits up to 600 s for the same lock and otherwise leaves its files to this
job.
.TP
.B token
Create the shift token for a data month if it does not exist:
.B darpa-spill-shift token \-\-dist
.I shift.dist
.B \-\-sigma
.I shift.sigma
.B \-\-cut
.I shift.cut
.B \-o
.IR FILE .
An existing token is never touched.
.RS
.TP
.BI \-\-month " YYYY\-MM"
That month (default: the current local month).
.TP
.B \-\-next
Next month; cron runs this on the 25th.
.RE
.TP
.B creds
With
.B KRB5CCNAME
set to
.BR credentials.krb5ccname :
read the ticket with
.BR klist ;
if there is none, alert
.B kinit needed
with the exact command
.BI "kinit \-r 7d \-c " "CACHE PRINCIPAL" ;
if it expires within
.B credentials.renew_below_hours
run
.BI "kinit \-R \-c " CACHE ;
warn and alert when the renewable lifetime ends within
.BR credentials.warn_renew_until_hours .
Then run, inside
.BR fdp.env_script ,
.PP
.RS
.RS
.nf
htgettoken \-a htvaultprod.fnal.gov \-i amsc \-r novaendwrite \e
    \-\-minsecs=3600 \-\-nooidc \-o FDP_TOKEN_FILE
.fi
.RE
.PP
If
.B credentials.env_file
holds
.B FERMI_USER
and
.B FERMI_PASSWORD
with real values (not empty, not
.BR CHANGEME ),
they are passed in that process's environment only, and
.B \-\-nooidc
is replaced by
.BI "\-\-ssoapprove=password \-\-ssocookiefile=" FILE
so Vault may fall back to SSO password approval.  The password and token
contents are never logged.  Kerberos expiry, renew-until, the bearer token's
expiry (decoded from its JWT payload) and the age of
.RI /tmp/vt_u UID
are recorded and shown on the status page.
.RE
.TP
.B status
Re-render
.I index.html
and
.I status.json
only.  The page refreshes itself every 60 s and shows: last run, result and
duration per job; credentials; ingest watermark and lag per source; disk
usage of
.BR data_root ;
a grid of the last 48 hours and 14 days by stream, each cell
.BR missing ", " partial ", " dumped ", " shifted ,
.B raw\-up
(raw uploaded),
.B public
(raw and shifted uploaded) or
.BR error ;
totals; pending uploads; the last 20 errors.
.TP
.B check
Preflight: server
.I /api/health
reachable, CA bundle readable, data root writable, state and status
directories writable, token directory mode 0700 (no group or other bits),
.B darpa-spill-dump
and
.B darpa-spill-shift
executable,
.B fdp.env_script
present,
.B fdp\-put \-\-version
and
.B htgettoken
runnable through it, mail command found, disk below
.BR alerts.disk_warn_percent .
Prints one line per check
.RB ( ok " or " FAIL );
exit status 1 if any fails.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIFILE\fP"
YAML configuration.  Default:
.BR $DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_CONFIG ,
else
.I ~/DARPA/publish/publish.yaml
if it exists, else the built-in defaults.
.TP
.BI \-\-set " SECTION.KEY=VALUE"
Override one setting; repeatable.  Top-level keys are written without a
section
.RB ( \-\-set\ timezone=UTC ).
Lists as
.B a,b,c
or
.BR "[a, b, c]" .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print every command that would change something (secrets redacted) instead of
running it, and write nothing: no files, no locks, no status page.  The state
database is copied into memory, so the decisions are those a real run would
make.  The ingest gate is read once and reported, not waited for.  Read-only
probes
.RB ( klist ,
the
.B check
commands,
.IR /api/sources )
still run.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log every step.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Log only warnings and errors.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Help; after a subcommand, that subcommand's options.
.PP
Write relative times with
.BR = :
.BR \-\-start=\-2d ,
or the leading minus is taken for an option.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Precedence, highest first: command line
.RB ( \-\-set ),
environment, the
.B .env
file named by
.BR paths.dotenv ,
the YAML file, built-in defaults.  Unknown keys are an error.  The example
.I config/publish.yaml
in the source tree holds exactly the defaults, with comments.
.PP
.RS
.nf
server:       url, ca_bundle, insecure (\-k), timeout (s, per request)
timezone:     America/Chicago
data_root:    /data/DARPA\-spilldata
formats:      [csv, json]
prefix:       nd
streams:      NAME: {signals: [...], types: [...], sources: [...]}
hourly:       catchup_hours (24)
daily:        catchup_days (3)
ingest_check: enabled, wait (300 s), poll (30 s), settle (60 s)
shift:        token_dir, token_name ("dts1\-{year}\-{month}.token"),
              dist (gauss), sigma (50us), cut (3sigma)
fdp:          enabled, env_script, ca_path, token_file, raw_dest,
              shifted_dest, parallel (\-j 4), timeout (3600 s), extra_args
credentials:  enabled, krb5ccname, principal, renew_below_hours (12),
              warn_renew_until_hours (24), env_file, htgettoken_args,
              sso_cookie_file, min_token_seconds (3600), timeout (300 s),
              sso_backoff_hours (12)
paths:        state_dir, status_dir, log_dir, dotenv
alerts:       enabled, email, mail_command (mailx), min_interval (3600 s),
              max_interval (86400 s), disk_warn_percent (85),
              stale_job_hours (3)
tools:        dump, shift  (empty: beside the running Python, else PATH),
              dump_timeout (7200 s per day of data in one call),
              shift_timeout (3600 s per file or token)
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B streams
replaces the default mapping as a whole.
.B fdp.enabled false
dumps and shifts only; files stay pending until it is turned on.
.B fdp.extra_args
is appended to every
.B fdp\-put
command; never put
.B \-\-replace
there.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/DARPA/publish/publish.yaml
Deployed configuration, read when no
.B \-c
or
.B DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_CONFIG
is given.
.TP
.I {data_root}/raw/ "\fR, \fP" {data_root}/shifted/
Local copies, kept indefinitely.
.TP
.I {state_dir}/publish.db
SQLite state: tables
.B files
(one row per raw file),
.B runs
(job, start, finish, result, JSON detail),
.B alerts
(last sent, mails sent and occurrences per condition) and
.B kv
(the SSO back-off, consecutive partial uploads per directory).
.TP
.IR {state_dir}/ JOB .lock ", " {state_dir}/dump.lock ", " {state_dir}/upload.lock
.BR flock (2)
locks.  Each holds the PID of its last holder; its mtime is when it was
taken.
.TP
.IR {status_dir}/index.html ", " {status_dir}/status.json
The status page, written atomically.
.TP
.I {log_dir}/
Where the crontab sends standard error.
.TP
.I {shift.token_dir}/dts1\-YYYY\-MM.token
One shift token per data month, mode 0600, in a 0700 directory.  Without it
that month's shifted files cannot be restored: back each one up.
.TP
.I fdp.token_file
The FDP bearer token written by
.BR creds .
.TP
.I credentials.env_file
.BR FERMI_USER " and " FERMI_PASSWORD
for SSO password approval; mode 0600.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_CONFIG
Configuration file, as
.BR \-c .
.TP
.BI DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_ SECTION _ KEY
Any setting, e.g.
.BR DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_FDP_ENABLED=0 ,
.BR DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_ALERTS_EMAIL=a@fnal.gov,b@fnal.gov .
Top-level settings have no section:
.BR DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_TIMEZONE ,
.BR DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_DATA_ROOT ,
.BR DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_PREFIX ,
.BR DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_FORMATS .
.TP
.BR DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_SERVER ", " _CA_BUNDLE ", " _INSECURE
Short forms of
.BR server.url ,
.BR server.ca_bundle ,
.BR server.insecure .
.TP
.BR DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_STATE_DIR ", " _STATUS_DIR ", " _LOG_DIR ", " _DOTENV
Short forms of the
.B paths
settings.
.PP
The
.B .env
file
.RB ( paths.dotenv )
holds
.I KEY=VALUE
lines; only
.B DARPA_SPILL_PUBLISH_*
keys are used.  It is separate from
.BR credentials.env_file .
.PP
Child processes:
.B KRB5CCNAME
is set for
.BR klist ", " kinit " and " htgettoken ;
.B FDP_TOKEN_DIR
and
.B FDP_TRANSFER_CA_PATH
for
.BR fdp\-put .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure: nothing could be done, a preflight check failed, or the state
database cannot be opened.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or configuration error (bad option, unknown key, bad timezone, bad
.BR \-\-month / \-\-date ).
.TP
.B 3
Partial: some steps succeeded and some failed; see the log, the status page
and
.BR last_error .
.TP
.B 75
Temporary: ingest has not reached the end of a piece, the same job is
already running, or another job held the dump lock.  cron's next run tries
again.
.PP
.B fdp\-put
exit codes recorded per file: 0 ok, 4 authentication, 6 exists, 7
network/TLS, 9 checksum mismatch, 12 partial, plus 124 timed out and 125
.B fdp.env_script
unreadable.  After 4, 7, 124 or 125 the upload stage stops (every further
call would fail the same way); after 12 nothing is marked uploaded (fdp\-put
does not say which files went), the rows note the partial, and the next
.B upload
run, with
.BR \-\-skip\-existing ,
sends the rest; a directory partial on two runs in a row is mailed.
.PP
Every subprocess runs in its own process group with a timeout
.RB ( tools.dump_timeout ,
.BR tools.shift_timeout ,
.BR fdp.timeout ,
.BR credentials.timeout );
on timeout the whole group is sent SIGTERM, then SIGKILL, and the step is
recorded with exit 124.
.PP
After
.B htgettoken
fails in SSO password mode, that mode is not tried again for
.B credentials.sso_backoff_hours
(or until
.B credentials.env_file
changes), so a wrong password is not replayed every 30 minutes and cannot
lock the account.
.SH EXAMPLES
The crontab on the gateway (user novadaq; from
.IR deploy/gateway/crontab.fragment ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
3,33 * * * *        run\-publish.sh creds
10 * * * *          run\-publish.sh hourly
30 0 * * *          run\-publish.sh daily
0 9 25 * *          run\-publish.sh token \-\-next
7,22,37,52 * * * *  run\-publish.sh upload
*/5 * * * *         run\-publish.sh status \-q
.fi
.RE
.PP
where
.I run\-publish.sh
sets
.B PATH
and the locale and runs the venv's
.B darpa\-spill\-publish \-c ~/DARPA/publish/publish.yaml
with the arguments given; each line appends to
.IR {log_dir}/ JOB .log.
.PP
Preflight, then see what the next hourly run would do:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa\-spill\-publish check
darpa\-spill\-publish \-n hourly
.fi
.RE
.PP
Redo one hour that failed, the second (CST) 01:00 of 1 November 2026:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa\-spill\-publish \-v hourly \-\-hour 2026\-11\-01T01\-0600
.fi
.RE
.PP
Redo one day:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa\-spill\-publish \-v daily \-\-date 2026\-10\-21
.fi
.RE
.PP
Backfill the archive from its start, without uploading yet, then upload:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa\-spill\-publish \-v backfill \-\-start 2026\-09\-17 \-\-end now \-\-no\-upload
darpa\-spill\-publish \-v upload
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only the daily files of September:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa\-spill\-publish backfill \-\-start 2026\-09\-01 \-\-end 2026\-09\-30 \-\-no\-hourly
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create next month's token now; dump without uploading:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa\-spill\-publish token \-\-next
darpa\-spill\-publish \-\-set fdp.enabled=false hourly
.fi
.RE
.PP
Restore a shifted file with the token of its data month:
.PP
.RS
.nf
darpa\-spill\-shift invert \-\-token\-file ~/anorman\-fdp/tokens/dts1\-2026\-10.token \e
    /data/DARPA\-spilldata/shifted/oct/2026\-oct\-22/nd\-all\-2026\-10\-22T13\-0500.shifted.csv \e
    \-o /tmp/nd\-all\-2026\-10\-22T13\-0500.csv
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR darpa-spill-dump (1),
.BR darpa-spill-shift (1),
.BR darpa-spill-server (1),
.BR darpa-spill-query (1),
.BR crontab (5),
.BR klist (1),
.BR kinit (1)
.PP
.IR docs/GATEWAY_OPERATIONS.md ,
the operator runbook, and
.IR docs/deployment/GATEWAY_DEPLOYMENT_PLAN.md ,
the design record (decisions D1\-D18), in the source tree.
